    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Michael Platt is a British billionaire investor and co-founder of BlueCrest Capital Management. Born in Preston and educated at the London School of Economics, he developed his trading career at J.P. Morgan. BlueCrest became a private investment partnership, managing capital for its partners and employees after returning outside investors' money.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Investor Michael Platt built an art collection by financing new work through All Visual Arts, the venture he established with art dealer Joe La Placa. Platt supplied capital while La Placa selected artists. Their model covered production costs in return for works retained in the collection, with other pieces sold and the profits shared.\n\nThe venture supported Paul Fryer's exhibition Let There Be More Light, staged across a former London church and a gallery. Its sculptures combined religious imagery with scientific machinery, including a wooden replica of a V2 rocket. All Visual Arts also worked with artists using charcoal, feathers and resin, extending the collection across different materials.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 92,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Direct founders’ interview and contemporary exhibition coverage document financing and its specific creative outcomes."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S5",
      "S6",
      "S7"
    ],
    "word_count": 108,
    "html": "<p>Investor Michael Platt built an art collection by financing new work through All Visual Arts, the venture he established with art dealer Joe La Placa. Platt supplied capital while La Placa selected artists. Their model covered production costs in return for works retained in the collection, with other pieces sold and the profits shared.</p>\r\n<p>The venture supported Paul Fryer's exhibition Let There Be More Light, staged across a former London church and a gallery. Its sculptures combined religious imagery with scientific machinery, including a wooden replica of a V2 rocket. All Visual Arts also worked with artists using charcoal, feathers and resin, extending the collection across different materials.</p>\r\n"
  },
